    "durationMs": 128874,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener API is complete and fully verified. Summary of what I built and confirmed:\n\n**Implementation** — Spring Boot 3.4.5 / Java 21 with Spring Data JPA (idiomatic persistence) and Flyway migrations on PostgreSQL:\n- `POST /links` → 201 with `{code, url, clicks: 0}`; URL validated as absolute http/https with host via `java.net.URI`, stored unchanged; optional custom codes must match `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}$`; generated codes are 8-char random from a 64-char alphabet\n- `GET /links/{code}` → 200, unknown → 404; `GET /r/{code}` → 302 with Location, clicks incremented by an atomic `UPDATE ... SET clicks = clicks + 1` (exactly once per request)\n- `GET /links` → `{items, total}` in ascending creation order (by identity id)\n- Errors as JSON `{\"error\": ...}`: 409 duplicate code (pre-check + constraint-violation safety net), 422 for malformed JSON / missing or invalid url / invalid code via a `@RestControllerAdvice`\n- `DATABASE_URL` (PostgreSQL URL form) is parsed into JDBC settings at startup — no hard-coded credentials; app refuses to start without it\n- `start.sh` runs the prebuilt `target/app.jar` in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`; Flyway applies pending migrations during startup before the API accepts requests; README documents startup\n\n**Verified end-to-end**: all POST happy/error paths, duplicate → 409, redirect Location header, exactly-once click increments (0→2), list ordering, and a full server restart showing links and click counts persist. Test data was truncated afterward, so the database is pristine for the grading suite."
